  "answer": "<p>The correct choice is <b>Implement a custom CloudWatch metric tracking the time from instance launch until it successfully passes an Elastic Load Balancer health check, and periodically update the Auto Scaling group's PauseTime and CoolDown settings so that they exceed this observed time.</b> This approach ensures that scaling decisions are based on when instances are actually ready to serve traffic, not just when they are launched. By aligning the PauseTime and CoolDown intervals with the real warm-up time, you prevent new instances from being considered unhealthy (and thus replaced prematurely) or excessive scaling from occurring.</p><p>The option <i>Increase the PauseTime and CoolDown periods on your Auto Scaling group above the instance's BootTime metric, using standard CloudWatch monitoring.</i> does not fully solve the problem, as BootTime alone does not represent when the instance is ready to serve load balancer traffic.&nbsp;The application's readiness is a key factor; using only BootTime may still trigger premature scaling.</p><p>As for <i>Set up a third-party configuration management solution using the AWS SDK to suspend all Auto Scaling ScheduledActions during peak traffic, resuming them after the period of high demand is over.</i>—this is not a recommended or scalable approach, as it interferes with auto scaling's dynamic nature and best practices by preventing necessary scaling actions.</p><p>Lastly, <i>Create a custom CloudWatch metric for the period from instance launch to when it passes an ELB health check, then adjust the UpdatePolicy's Pause Time and reduce the Scaling Adjustment by half.</i>&nbsp;adjusts the scaling adjustment without any clear reason that it would address the underlying issue and could end up under-provisioning resources.</p><p><b>Tip:</b>&nbsp;Always base scaling pause and cooldown values on actual instance readiness for load balancing, not just on their launch time. Custom metrics can provide the required precision for optimal scaling behavior.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your company has a CloudFormation-based multi-tier system that uses an Auto Scaling group to power its web tier, which serves client traffic through a load balancer. During a traffic surge, you notice that new EC2 instances are being added at a much faster rate than the increased traffic would justify, given the scaling policies in place. What is the most appropriate adjustment to make sure instance scaling aligns more closely with real workload demands?</p>",
